From: Reindert Pelsma <reindert.pelsma@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 21 Aug 2026 23:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] sdl2: grab mode owns the input, and only when asked to

0008 makes the SDL backend show the guest.  This makes its grab mode work, which
is what pointer lock needs -- and pointer lock is the difference between a VM
you can compute in and one you can play a game in.

The core of it is one call.  sdl_hide_cursor() asks for
SDL_SetRelativeMouseMode() only when the current pointing device is relative,
and nvkvm's guest has a tablet current, so the one backend that CAN take a real
Wayland lock never asked for it.  Switching the device on grab is what engages
it.  That matters because QEMU's GTK backend cannot do this at all: it emulates
a lock with gdk_seat_grab() plus gdk_device_warp(), and an X11 client under
Xwayland can do neither, because the compositor owns the pointer.  SDL asks
properly -- SDL_SetRelativeMouseMode() is zwp_pointer_constraints_v1 plus
zwp_relative_pointer_v1, the same protocols a native game uses.

Making it usable took five more fixes, all found by using it on real hardware,
and all from ONE root cause: gui_grab conflates "this window is hiding the host
cursor" with "the guest owns your input".  Those were the same thing until a
real pointer lock existed.  Upstream sets gui_grab from absolute_mouse_grab() on
every window-enter, for the first meaning only.  Once a lock was possible, every
test of that flag was ambiguous, and each was wrong differently:

  * THE ESCAPE DID NOT WORK.  ctrl-alt-g released, then hovering re-locked:
    ungrabbing restores the tablet, which makes qemu_input_is_absolute() true,
    which makes WINDOWEVENT_ENTER grab again, which became a lock.  No way back
    to normal mode.  sdl_grab_start() now takes user_initiated, and only an
    explicit grab locks -- ctrl-alt-g, the fullscreen toggle, -full-screen.
    Window-enter, mouse-motion and left-click do not, so a click stays a click.

  * THE KEYBOARD WAS NEVER GRABBED.  SDL_SetWindowGrab() is mouse-only
    (SDL_SetWindowMouseGrab is its alias) and win32_kbd_set_grab() is a no-op off
    Windows, so a grabbed guest never saw Super or Alt-Tab.  Added
    SDL_SetWindowKeyboardGrab(), which on Wayland is
    zwp_keyboard_shortcuts_inhibit_manager_v1.  Explicit grabs only.

  * CTRL-ALT-G NEEDED TWO PRESSES.  The hotkey tested gui_grab, which the
    incidental grab had already set, so the first press ungrabbed THAT.  It
    toggles on the lock now: one press, one toggle.

  * NORMAL MODE LEAKED INPUT TO THE GUEST.  SDL_SetWindowGrab() was taken on a
    mere hover, and it is a mouse CAPTURE: motion kept arriving after the
    pointer had left the window, and clicks over an unrelated host application
    were forwarded to the guest.  A privacy problem, not just a wrong pointer.
    Capture is now taken only for an explicit grab; without it the compositor
    delivers motion only while the pointer is genuinely over the window, and
    withholds it over whatever is stacked above.

  * THE GUEST POINTER STOPPED SHORT OF THE EDGE.  x and y arrive in WINDOW
    coordinates and were scaled against surface_width(), the guest scanout.
    Whenever the window is not exactly the scanout size the axis is declared
    longer than the coordinates can reach, so the guest pointer covered only
    window/scanout of the screen.  Scaled against the window now.  Only absolute
    mode can have this: grab mode sends deltas, which carry no coordinate space.

  * The title bar offered "Press Ctrl-Alt-G to exit grab" while merely hovering,
    with nothing to exit.  The caption tracks the lock.

The escape survives the keyboard grab: ctrl-alt-g is matched by QEMU's own key
handler before anything reaches the guest, so inhibiting the compositor cannot
trap the user.  Every design in this area -- QEMU's own, VirtualBox's host key,
the browser Pointer Lock API -- depends on that invariant.

Verified interactively on an RTX 3050 and an RTX 4070, both GNOME/Wayland:
normal mode has a seamless pointer that tracks to all four edges at any window
size, stops at the window, respects stacking and leaves host shortcuts alone;
grab mode locks the pointer, hides the cursor and forwards Super to the guest;
one keypress each way; caption correct in both.

This is deliberately one patch and not a patch plus corrections.  The series is
applied in order by scripts/build_qemu.sh, whose idempotency test is
`git apply --reverse --check` per patch -- and that test breaks when a later
patch edits lines an earlier one added, because the earlier patch no longer
reverses cleanly.  A dependent patch would make re-running the build fail on an
already-patched tree.
